<PageLayout title='Computational Natural History' kicker="RESEARCH" lead='Natural history has always been about observing patterns. Computation lets us connect more of them.'>
<section><div class="prose content-page"><p>This line brings together biodiversity, phylogeny, geography, fossils, genomic data and scientific storytelling. The objective is not to replace field natural history, but to build digital ways of navigating biological knowledge across scales.</p><p>BIOATLAS and Darwin's Monsters grow from this idea: scientific interfaces should allow people to move from an organism to its evolutionary history, geography, traits, relatives and genomic context without flattening biology into a list of facts.</p><h2>$ outputs</h2><ul><li>Interactive biodiversity maps</li><li>Phylogenetic exploration</li><li>Species-centered data stories</li><li>Educational simulations and scientific interfaces</li><li>Open digital collections that connect research and public curiosity</li></ul></div></section>
</PageLayout>